'''''Sedum anglicum''''', the '''English stonecrop''',<ref>{{cite book |author1=David Chapman |title=Exploring the Cornish Coast |date=2008 |publisher=Alison Hodge |location=[[Penzance]] |isbn=9780906720561 |page=105}}</ref> is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the genus ''[[Sedum]]'' in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Crassulaceae]].

==Description==
''Sedum anglicum'' is a low-growing perennial with stubby, succulent, untoothed, alternate leaves. These are often greyish-green, and may turn pink in dry conditions. The flowers are short-stalked and star-like, white (sometimes tinged pink), with ten contrasting stamens and five carpels. The fruits are red.<ref>McClintock, D, and R.S.R. Fitter. ''The Pocket Guide to Wild Flowers.'' Collins, London</ref>

==Distribution and habitat==
''Sedum anglicum'' occurs in western Europe, including Norway, Sweden, Ireland, Great Britain, France, Portugal and Spain.[http://linnaeus.nrm.se/flora/di/crassula/sedum/seduangv.jpg] It is usually found on dry rocks, walls and sand dunes, often near the sea.<ref>[http://www.habitas.org.uk/flora/species.asp?item=3472 Flora of Northern Ireland]</ref> It prefers thin, acidic soils and thrives in rock crevices and on cliffs, and also grows inland on walls and hedge banks.<ref>[http://www.guernsey.net/~cdavid/botany/files/sedum%20anglicum/ ''Sedum anglicum'']</ref> In [[Great Britain]], it is much more common in the west of the country than in the east.<ref>[http://bsbi.org/maps?taxonid=2cd4p9h.ckd BSBI Maps Scheme]</ref>

